Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 18:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] color: extrapolate the inverse shaper past its range

calcColorTransform places the 3D LUT grid in shaper space and finds each
grid point's source value with ApplyLut1D_Inverse_Linear, which clamps to
the shaper's last entry. A Gamma 2.2 -> PQ shaper for SDR at 203 nits ends
at PQ 0.581, so every grid point above it holds SDR white and the cell
below interpolates toward that plateau. SDR white leaves the LUT at PQ
0.568 (about 181 nits), and every bright colour dims the same way. The
same LUT pair drives HDR scanout of SDR content.

Continue the shaper's last segment past its end instead. Grid points above
the range then hold the colour they stand for, and SDR white lands within
1.5 10-bit codes of 203 nits. Shapers that reach 1.0 (PQ -> Gamma 2.2, PQ
passthrough) never look past their end and do not change.
---
 src/color_helpers.cpp | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/color_helpers.cpp b/src/color_helpers.cpp
index 64430a5f..11c7bef4 100644
--- a/src/color_helpers.cpp
+++ b/src/color_helpers.cpp
@@ -476,6 +476,11 @@ inline float FindLutInv(const float * start,
     // regardless of whether the original LUT was increasing or decreasing because
     // this function uses std::lower_bound().
 
+    // Past the last entry, continue the last segment. A 3D LUT grid point above the shaper's
+    // range still bounds the cell below it, so it needs the source value that point stands for.
+    if ( val > *end && end > start && *end > *( end - 1 ) )
+        return ( (float)( end - start ) + startOffset + ( val - *end ) / ( *end - *( end - 1 ) ) ) * scale;
+
     // Clamp the value to the range of the LUT.
     const float cv = std::min( std::max( val, *start ), *end );
 
